>> title: Siu Mai
>> category: Dumplings & Rolls
>> tags: pork, shrimp, dumpling, chinese, steamed
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: shu mai, shumai, siu maai, shao mai, siomai, 燒賣, pork and shrimp dumpling
>> pairs-with: har-gow
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr 15 min
>> step.1: soak the mushrooms 30 min, squeeze dry, dice fine
>> step.2: stir one way until sticky, chill 20 min
>> step.3: press each wrapper into a cup, pack it full, flatten the top
>> step.4: steam 8 min

Soak @dried shiitake mushrooms{4} in @hot water{1%cup}(240 mL) for ~soak{30%min}, then squeeze them out and dice them fine. The soaking liquor is worth keeping for a pot of rice.

Stir @ground pork{10%oz}(285 g; coarse, and not lean — a third fat) with @raw shrimp{5%oz}(140 g; peeled and roughly chopped), @&(~1)mushrooms{}, @light soy sauce{1%Tbs}, @oyster sauce{2%tsp}, @Shaoxing wine{2%tsp}, @toasted sesame oil{2%tsp}, @sugar{1%tsp}, @cornstarch{1%Tbs}, @fresh ginger{2%tsp}(grated) and @white pepper{1/4%tsp} in one direction until the mixture holds together in a mass, then ~chill{20%min}.

Cup @round wonton wrappers{24}(thin and yellow) one at a time in your hand, pack @&(~1)filling{} in with a butter knife until it stands proud of the rim, and flatten the top — the sides are pleated by the hand, not by folding, and the top is left open.

Steam @&(~1)dumplings{} in a #bamboo steamer{} lined with perforated parchment, each one dotted with @carrot{2%tsp}(finely diced, standing in for crab roe), over boiling water for ~steam{8%min}.
